{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q17",
  "speechText": "In one case, we had a problem with large PDF processing jobs. During OCR, the memory usage kept increasing, and eventually the worker crashed. The first thing I wanted to do was to find the root cause. I did not want to assume that the worker itself was the real problem. For tracking, I keep clear logs with the important IDs, status, errors, and performance metrics, such as latency, processing time, and memory usage. This helps me monitor the system, find what went wrong, and debug problems more easily. Then, I traced the whole processing flow. This helped me see which batch or page was slow, and when the memory usage became too high. From that, I found that the main bottleneck was the OCR CPU inference. Based on that, I reduced the batch size and adjusted the settings for the CPU path. I also used separate settings for the CPU path and the GPU path, so the system could still run reliably on CPU before the GPU service was deployed. For recovery, I added checkpoint and recovery logic. So if one step failed, we could continue from the last checkpoint instead of restarting the whole job. Then, I also worked on the infrastructure side together with DevOps to move the heavy OCR inference to a remote GPU service. So overall, I did not only focus on stopping the crash. I also checked worker stability, memory usage, job completion, and processing time to make sure the new solution was stable and safe to roll out.",
